knowledge card:
{
  "variable": "baseline caregiver age",
  "knowledge card": "Type": "demographic/clinical baseline variable",
  "knowledge card": "Description: 'Baseline caregiver age' refers to the age of a caregiver at the start of a study, assessment, or intervention. It is recorded as an initial demographic characteristic to describe the caregiver population and to support comparison of outcomes over time. In research and clinical documentation, baseline indicates the first measurement taken before follow-up or treatment."
}

{
  "variable": "baseline emotional support",
  "knowledge card": "Type": "psychosocial baseline variable. Description: 'Baseline emotional support' refers to the level or availability of emotional support a person has at the initial assessment or start of a study or care period. It typically reflects support from family, friends, caregivers, or social networks that provides comfort, encouragement, empathy, and help with coping before any intervention or follow-up."
}

{
  "variable": "baseline breastfeeding method",
  "knowledge card": "Type": "clinical/demographic feeding variable",
  "knowledge card": "Description: 'Baseline breastfeeding method' refers to the way an infant is being fed at the initial or reference time point of a clinical assessment or study. It typically records whether feeding is direct breastfeeding, expressed breast milk, mixed feeding, or another method. This variable is used to describe starting feeding practices before follow-up or intervention."
}

{
  "variable": "baseline residence",
  "knowledge card": "Type": "demographic / living arrangement variable",
  "knowledge card": "Type: demographic / living arrangement variable. Description: 'Baseline residence' refers to the place or type of residence where a person lives at the start of a study, clinical evaluation, or intervention. It may describe geographic location, household setting, or residential category such as private home, institution, or temporary housing, and is used as an important background characteristic."
}
